now a truly inspirational story about um  somebody who broke their neck and spine  and former rugby player ed jackson is  uh a first quadriplegic to reach a  summit of 7 000 feet along with three  others ed climbed to the summit of mount  himlang himmel  i hope i've pronounced that right in  nepal let's find out he's with us now hi  ed  are you well  i am very well is that how you pronounce  the mountain  seven thousand meters uh not feet which  is about twenty two thousand feet  um so  it's like a slight difference but um  especially when you were in the last  7000 feet of it um tell me how it went  um  it was an unbelievable trip i mean we  were out there for nearly a month um  completely off-grid for two weeks so  it's one of the most remote mountains in  the himalayas so there's no signal wi-fi  as you can imagine which was a pretty  special process in itself just us and  the porters and sherpas the nepali team  in tents for two weeks  on the actual mountain  the climbing period um  was difficult to say the least because  no one's been on that mountain for over  two years um it's a pretty  full-on seventh thousand meter mountain  and and it goes over a lot of glaciers  so um the crevasse fields have moved  quite a lot in those two years so a lot  of the time we were having to feel our  way  through it um we ended up with a 1200  meter summit day vertical meter summit  day which um which is a lot because camp  3 wasn't able to be set up and we  actually ended up getting to about 6 800  meters  which is a new world record for for  someone with a  spinal cord injury but it was um it was  very treacherous coming back down and  actually by the time we got back to the  crevasse field at about 6 000 meters  the snow had softened up um and people  were just falling through crevasses in  our team we lost our lead guide to uh  through a crevasse that was about two  stories deep we managed to rescue him  but then we had to take the decision to  call a helicopter rescue because we  couldn't move anywhere without falling  through a crevasse um  and at that point at six pm we were  about sort of 18 19 hours in uh the  helicopter said sorry we can't come and  fly at night so you're gonna have to  survive the night on the mountain so we  spent  a night at 6 000 meters with no tent no  food no water um just trying to survive  in minus 20 degrees  uh  so we're very lucky to be honest to to  have made it through um  an unbelievable achievement i'm very  proud of for the whole team to the  height we got to but a bit of bit an uh  rather more exciting end than we  anticipated  now you you spinal injury was uh after  you dived into a shallow pool i believe  and you damaged your your neck and your  spine but you know we've seen what  you've done as a result how can people  um support the sort of work that you do  and get involved  yeah um it's a good question so i i when  i had my spinal cord injury obviously i  was professional player before it was  very difficult for me  losing the ability to move and um i was  a physical person and my identity was  wrapped up in that and you know my  spinal cord injury still affects me  greatly all the weakness through my left  side i have underlying  um conditions sort of bladder bowel  temperature regulation spasms all of  these things that aren't very conducive  to climbing mountains but i got to the  point where  i was just constantly being told what i  couldn't do anymore  and i kind of got i had enough of that  and i just started to explore what i  could do and it started off by hobbling  up small hills and it's transpired into  climbing bigger mountains but  hopefully we can represent i want to  represent the the entire spinal cord  injury community but not just them  anyone with physical limitations to the  outdoors because i realize how lucky i  am to even be in that position to to  take on a mountain like that so many  people aren't every injury is different  um but hopefully by doing what we're  doing we can encourage more people to  maybe break out of the narrative that  they're being given being told they  can't do things and go and find out for  themselves so i set up a charity to do  exactly that to help other people who  are going through trauma use the  mountains use the outdoors to encourage  them to do that call millimeters to  mountains so people can follow that on  social media or my account at jackson 8  for more information um  but also i've been working with  burkhouse around adapting kit to make  the outdoor more accessible for people  with physical limitations because i  massively believe in the healing power  of nature a bit ironic considering i'm  sat here with like cold burned on my lip  and having spent a night night on the  mountain but to be honest um i don't  regret anything that happened over there  it was an incredible experience and  one i probably would never have  been through if it wasn't for that  supposedly terrible day when i had my  accident and my life changed forever and  i just hope that other people who go  through trauma and life-changing  accidents or incidents can start to see  the brightness on the other side and  stop looking at things they can't do and  start dreaming about the things they can  and take that first step towards um  towards a new life really  okay ed amazing story and well done you  i'm glad you all got back safely thank  you very much indeed thanks a lot thanks
 

